<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ségur Ordinance</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1781">1781</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ségur Ordinance was a French regulation of 1781 that required candidates for officer commissions to prove four generations of nobility in the male line, or to be the son of an officer holding the Cross of Saint-Louis. Named after the minister of war Philippe Henri de Ségur, who had advised against it, it was approved by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> for the French infantry, cavalry and dragoons and extended in 1784 to the hussars, mounted chasseurs and foreign infantry regiments. Rich commoners and nobles of the robe could no longer buy their way in as officers. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-estate">Third Estate</a>'s outcry and the resentment of the robe nobility fed the crisis that led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Before the Revolution French society was divided into three estates, and the Second Estate, the nobility, into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobles-of-the-sword">nobility of the sword</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobles-of-the-robe">nobility of the robe</a>. The sword nobility descended from the chivalric nobility of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, lived on fairly static incomes from land and military commissions, dominated the court and regarded advancement in the army as the highest aristocratic achievement.</p><p>The robe nobility were financiers, merchants and property owners whose newer status came from wealth, often through personal success or family connections, and who lacked the ornate genealogies of the court nobility. Many had lent money to the government at high interest. As their families became established, young robe nobles bought their way to high ranks in the army, angering sword nobles who wished to keep the officer corps exclusive.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Its official title stated that no one could be proposed for a sub-lieutenancy without proof of four generations of nobility, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> approved it on 22 May 1781. Commoners and robe nobles who wished to serve in the branches it covered now had to rise through the ranks like other roturiers, a hard and uncertain course of one or two decades: a commoner who entered between 14 and 18 could usually not become a second lieutenant before his late twenties or mid-thirties, and field or general rank became hard or impossible to reach. The artillery and engineers stayed outside the rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Many in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-estate">Third Estate</a> saw the ordinance as a way for the nobility to shut the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a> out of army prestige, although its original purpose was only to exclude other nobles. It also fixed the robe nobility in an uneasy position: rich and noble, yet without the long male lines needed for acceptance among the court nobles. The resentment of both groups shaped political allegiances in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> of 1789–91.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ségur Ordinance” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the late 18th century the doors began to close: the old nobility resented the newcomers, and the <b>Ségur Ordinance</b> of 1781 made new infantry and cavalry officers prove four generations of noble descent, so that a commoner had to spend ten or twenty years in the ranks…</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancien-regime-france">Social Hierarchy of Ancien Régime France</a></li></ul></section>
